<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Apple has required this file for App Store submission since 2024, and an
	  upload without it is rejected or flagged. It is also the one place where
	  what we tell Apple has to match what we tell readers on /privacy, so it is
	  written from that page rather than from a template.

	  Rewritten 2026-08-27 on Hidde's ask ("maak de privacy labels kloppend"),
	  because it had not moved since accounts were only an email address. Three
	  things changed under it: a display name and a profile photograph (his yes,
	  2026-08-26), a follow graph, and crash diagnostics (his yes, 2026-08-27).
	  A manifest that describes last month's app is worse than none, because it
	  is a statement to Apple that happens to be false.

	  Corrected again 2026-08-28. Two entries here were false and had been since
	  the sightings sync landed on 08-27: this file said photographs of trees
	  never leave the phone and that a position is never sent anywhere, while
	  SightingSync.push uploads the photograph to the sightings bucket and
	  writes a row carrying user_id, lat, lng, note and taken_at. Sightings.swift
	  had its own version of that stale sentence fixed the same day and this one
	  was missed. It is the same lesson twice: a comment describing last month's
	  app is a statement to Apple that happens to be false, and this one is in
	  Hidde's name.

	  What is deliberately NOT declared as collected, because "collected" in
	  Apple's sense means leaving the device, and these do not:

	  The map's own reading of where you are, which opens it on the trees near
	  you and is forgotten. What IS sent is the position saved WITH a tree you
	  record yourself, and that is declared below as precise location.

	  The tick list of somebody who has not signed in. That lives in
	  UserDefaults on the phone and goes nowhere until an account exists.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- Only for people who make an account, and only to sign them in. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- The display name somebody chooses. It does not have to be their
			     real one and the app says so where it is asked for, but Apple's
			     category is Name and under-declaring is the worse mistake. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- Two kinds, both linked to the account and neither shown to
			     anybody else. The profile picture goes to the avatars bucket
			     under the account's own id and is shown beside the name. A
			     photograph of a tree you recorded goes to the sightings bucket,
			     which is private and has no policy anywhere letting one account
			     read another's: it is there so a lost phone does not take your
			     trees with it. Signed out, neither leaves the device. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- Where a tree you recorded yourself was standing, saved with that
			     record so your own map can show it and a new phone can get it
			     back. Nothing streams: one coordinate per tree you record, at
			     the moment you record it, and only once you have signed in. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- The account's own id, which is what every row here hangs off and
			     what makes deleting an account able to take all of it. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- The collection itself: which trees you saved and ticked off,
			     synced to the account so a new phone does not lose it. Also who
			     follows whom, what somebody sends through the contribute form,
			     and a report about another account. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- Crashes and hangs, from MetricKit (Kit/Diagnostics.swift). NOT
			     LINKED, and that is a property of the data rather than a claim:
			     the row carries the payload, the app version and the OS version,
			     and nothing that says whose phone it came from. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCrashData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- Hangs and launch times arrive in the same payloads as crashes.
			     Apple's own category for that is Performance Data. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePerformanceData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- Which screens get opened and which of the four verbs get used,
			     added 2026-08-30 on Hidde's yes to a measurement tool. Six
			     events, listed in Kit/Measure.swift, sent to our own PostHog
			     project on their EU cloud.

			     NOT LINKED, and as with the crash data that is a property of
			     what is sent rather than a claim about our intentions: the row
			     carries an event name, the app version, the major OS version,
			     and an id that is a random UUID this phone made for itself. No
			     email, no account id, no coordinates, and no advertising
			     identifier. Signing in changes none of it.

			     NOT TRACKING, because none of it is joined to data from another
			     company or used to target advertising, which is what Apple's
			     word means here. That is also why this needs no ATT prompt.

			     The PURPOSE is Analytics rather than App Functionality, which
			     the entries above use. Nothing in the app works better because
			     of this; it exists so we can tell whether any of it is used.
			     Declaring it as functionality would be the flattering answer
			     and the false one. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- Saved.swift, Nudge.swift and Moderation.swift keep the
			     collection, the "have we already asked" flags and the blocked
			     list here. CA92.1 is the reason code for reading back only what
			     this app itself wrote. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
